We start by asking, "what can we multiply by $x-2$ to get $x^2-3x+k$ where $k$ is an integer?"

The answer is $x-1$:

$\blueD{(x-2)(x-1)=x^2-3x+2}$

Now we rewrite the numerator and split the quotient:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x^2-3x+9}{x-2}&=\dfrac{(\blueD{x^2-3x+2})+7}{x-2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-3x+2}}{x-2}+\dfrac{7}{x-2}
\end{align}$

Now we use the fact that $\blueD{(x-2)(x-1)=x^2-3x+2}$ to rewrite the numerator of the first quotient and cancel common factors:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-3x+2}}{x-2}+\dfrac{7}{x-2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{\blueD{(x-2)}}\blueD{(x-1)}}{\cancel{x-2}}+\dfrac{7}{x-2}
\\\\
&=x-1+\dfrac{7}{x-2}
\end{align}$
